A small MON document is one root record of literal values. This save record
shows the accepted shape: named fields, a nested record, a map, an explicit
`none` and ordinary string data.

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    format_version = 1,
    title = "Strategy",
    window = (width = 1280, height = 720),
    scores = {"Priya" = 10, "Rob" = 12},
    note = none,
]

The same record may carry explicit outer parentheses. A lone scalar,
collection or choice is not a document: put it in a named root field.
Encoding fills only exact missing field defaults and emits the completed
record in schema order, including explicit `none` and values equal to
defaults. See @#mon-format (MON format) for the complete literal-data
reference.

Strictness is the point. These inputs fail:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    -- INVALID: MON never evaluates expressions, including foldable ones
    width = 1280 + 0,

    -- INVALID: a template is producer syntax, not a MON string literal
    title = [: Strategy],

    -- INVALID: missing comma between entries
    title = "Strategy"
    width = 1280,

    -- INVALID: `{}` is a collection, never an empty map; use `{=}`
    scores = {},

    -- INVALID: duplicate keys are rejected, not merged
    scores = {"Priya" = 10, "Priya" = 12},

    -- INVALID: decimal spelling never converts to an Int target
    width = 1280.0,

    -- INVALID: unknown record fields fail, even with defaults nearby
    titl = "Strategy",

    -- INVALID: reserved user names also fail with underscores or different case
    __LoOp = 1,

    -- INVALID: the field label and `=` must share one logical line
    title
    = "Strategy",
]

Independent Rust callers use `moth_mon`. Compiler clients may use the same API
and Rust types through the convenience re-export `moth::mon`. Prepare a schema
once and reuse it across documents. The codec returns owned values or a
structured `MonError` that supports ordinary Rust reporting and propagation.
Moth-native operations, `$mon`, automatic schema extraction and the static
project builder remain deferred. Compiled Moth programs have no runtime codec
support.
